    <fr:title text="Permutation, Grand-Product, and Memory Arguments">Permutation, Grand-Product, and Memory Arguments</fr:title>
  </fr:frontmatter>
  <fr:mainmatter>
    <html:p>
    The arguments that enforce global consistency across a trace, as opposed to row-local constraints. <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003U/" title="Memory Checking in zkVMs" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003U/" display-uri="dhsorens-003U" type="local">Memory</fr:link> is the hard case and the usual source of unsoundness.
</html:p>
    <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Permutation and multiset-equality arguments; the grand product and its logUp replacement.</html:li>
        <html:li>Offline memory checking: reads and writes as multisets of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(address, value, timestamp)]]></fr:tex> tuples, and the conditions that make it sound.</html:li>
        <html:li>Address-sorted versus timestamp-sorted formulations, and what each costs.</html:li>
        <html:li>Read-write memory in the folding setting.</html:li>
        <html:li>The constraint that is always forgotten: monotonicity of timestamps, and what a missing range check on it permits.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
    <html:p>
    Sources: <fr:link href="https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1605" type="external">Nebula: efficient read-write memory</fr:link>, ArkLib's <html:code>ProofSystem/ConstraintSystem/MemoryChecking.lean</html:code>.
</html:p>
  </fr:mainmatter>
