                <fr:title text="AIR">AIR</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>
    The arithmetisation the STARK toolkits consume, and the entry point for the formalisation work: <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003I/" title="STARKs: Plonky3, Stwo, and Circle STARKs" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003I/" display-uri="dhsorens-003I" type="local">Plonky3</fr:link> and Stwo both take an AIR as input, and <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002Y/" title="Clean: Circuits and Their Specifications in Lean" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002Y/" display-uri="dhsorens-002Y" type="local">Clean</fr:link> targets it.
</html:p>
                <html:p>
    Questions this page should answer:
    <html:ul><html:li>Execution trace, transition constraints, boundary constraints; the uniformity assumption that makes the format succinct.</html:li>
        <html:li>Constraint degree, and how it propagates into the quotient polynomial and the <fr:link href="/dhsorens-002O/" title="Reed–Solomon and Linear Codes" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-002O/" display-uri="dhsorens-002O" type="local">rate</fr:link>.</html:li>
        <html:li>Preprocessed columns and randomised AIR with preprocessing (RAP) — what the extra randomness is for.</html:li>
        <html:li>Multiple tables and cross-table constraints; how a <fr:link href="/dhsorens-003T/" title="Trace Layout, Chips, and Tables" uri="https://derekhsorensen.com/dhsorens-003T/" display-uri="dhsorens-003T" type="local">zkVM's chips</fr:link> are expressed.</html:li>
        <html:li>Padding, and the soundness bugs that live in the boundary between padded and real rows.</html:li></html:ul></html:p>
